>> >Create Double (level 7, matter or clerical): A real (not illusionary)
>> >double of you is created beside you. It is wielding a weapon identical
>> >to yours, is dressed in the same armor, and is wearing the same
>> >rings/amulets, but has no other possessions. It has identical
>> >intrinsics and stats to those you possessed at the time of creation.
>> >It is tame and will fight any nearby monsters in melee combat, but
>> >cannot cast spells (as this would be difficult to code.) After a short
>> >amount of time (30-50 turns), or when it is killed, it vanishes,
>> >leaving nothing behind.
>> 
>> Again, a can of worms, I'd think; how would you handle
>> leprechaun/nymph/footrice attacks?
> 
>I would rather call this spell Simulacrum, after the AD&D spell that
>does this same thing.  In Nethack, it wouldn't be too unbalanced.  The
>only characters that could cast a spell this high level wouldn't be
>the combat-oriented characters anyway.  On the other hand, this could
>be an effect of a new, very rare scroll, which would allow even combat
>classes to create duplicates.
> 
>The duplicate could be treated like any other tame pet, with the
>exception that it and its inventory disappears whenever it would
>normally die.

I like the idea, but could the whole thing be simplified more by
creating the simulacrum _only_? We could ignore inventory and
intrinsics for the most part (thus no new monster classes/unique
monsters needed for each casting), and create a 'pet'
human/elf/dwarf/orc. Sort of like the create familiar spell, but with
a tougher familiar who disappears in a few hundred turns.

